the integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 0 \\leq n < 1399 \\) and \\( n \\) is the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399, we need to find \\( n \\) such that:\n\n\\[ 160n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\]\n\nThis means we need to find \\( n \\) such that \\( 160n - 1399k = 1 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). This is a linear Diophantine equation, and we can solve it using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 160 and 1399:\n\n1. \\( 1399 = 8 \\cdot 160 + 159 \\)\n2. \\( 160 = 1 \\cdot 159 + 1 \\)\n3. \\( 159 = 159 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\)\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"c453dc5efccf3083cad5f5df954ebfd2fef20c39376826eaec2698ecc307ba22"},"seconds":16.623834133148193,"seed":677984073878865790,"stop":"eos","text":"From the Euclidean Algorithm steps, we see that the gcd of 160 and 1399 is 1, which confirms that an inverse exists. Now, we backtrack to express 1 as a combination of 160 and 1399:\n\n1. From the second step, we have \\( 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot 159 \\).\n2. From the first step, we substitute \\( 159 = 1399 - 8 \\cdot 160 \\) into the equation above:\n\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot (1399 - 8 \\cdot 160) \\]\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1399 + 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 9 \\cdot 160 - 1399 \\]\n\nThus, we have \\( 9 \\cdot 160 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\). This shows that \\( n = 9 \\) is the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399.\n\nTherefore, the solution is \\(\\boxed{9}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{9}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":true},"graded_text":"From the Euclidean Algorithm steps, we see that the gcd of 160 and 1399 is 1, which confirms that an inverse exists. Now, we backtrack to express 1 as a combination of 160 and 1399:\n\n1. From the second step, we have \\( 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot 159 \\).\n2. From the first step, we substitute \\( 159 = 1399 - 8 \\cdot 160 \\) into the equation above:\n\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot (1399 - 8 \\cdot 160) \\]\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1399 + 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 9 \\cdot 160 - 1399 \\]\n\nThus, we have \\( 9 \\cdot 160 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\). This shows that \\( n = 9 \\) is the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399.\n\nTherefore, the solution is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).","id":"number_theory:231"}
